low landscaping under buildings, tracks, etc.: î€®BAllow landscaping under buildings and tracks without removing them3Allow more realistically sized catchment areas: î€®SHave differently sized catchment areas for different types of stations and airportsICompany stations can serve industries with attached neutral stations: î€®ÁOWhen enabled, industries with attached stations (such as Oil Rigs) may also be served by company-owned stations built nearby. When disabled, these industries may only be served by their attached stations. Any nearby company stations won't be able to serve them, nor will the attached station serve anything else other than the industry@Allow removal of more town-owned roads, bridges and tunnels: î€®@Make it easier to remove town-owned infrastructure and buildingsMaximum length of trains: î€® Set the maximum length of trainsî€µ tileî€¼␀␀␀s#Amount of vehicle smoke/sparks: î€®=Set how much smoke or how many sparks are emitted by vehiclesTrain acceleration model: î€®ÀòSelect the physics model for train acceleration. The "original" model penalises slopes equally for all vehicles. The "realistic" model penalises slopes and curves depending on various properties of the consist, like length and tractive effort$Road vehicle acceleration model: î€®ÀëSelect the physics model for road vehicle acceleration. The "original" model penalises slopes equally for all vehicles. The "realistic" model penalises slopes depending on various properties of the engine, for example 'tractive effort'Slope steepness for trains: î€®\Steepness of a sloped tile for a train. Higher values make it more difficult to climb a hillî€µ%&Slope steepness for road vehicles: î€®cSteepness of a sloped tile for a road vehicle. Higher values make it more difficult to climb a hill)Forbid trains from making 90Â° turns: î€®Àõ90 degree turns occur when a horizontal track is directly followed by a vertical track piece on the adjacent tile, thus making the train turn by 90 degree when traversing the tile edge instead of the usual 45 degrees for other track combinations1Allow to join stations not directly adjacent: î€®zAllow adding parts to a station without directly touching the existing parts, by Ctrl+Clicking while placing the new partsInflation: î€®UEnable inflation in the economy, where costs are slightly faster rising than paymentsMaximum bridge length: î€®#Maximum length for building bridgesMaximum bridge height: î€®#Maximum height for building bridgesMaximum tunnel length: î€®#Maximum length for building tunnels0Manual primary industry construction method: î€®ÁEMethod of funding a primary industry. 'none' means it is not possible to fund any, 'prospecting' means funding is possible, but construction occurs in a random spot on the map and may as well fail, 'as other industries' means raw industries can be constructed by companies like processing industries in any position they likeNoneAs other industriesProspecting Flat area around industries: î€®‰Amount of flat space around an industry. This ensures empty space will remain available around an industry for building tracks, et cetera/Allow multiple similar industries per town: î€®™Normally, a town does not want more than one industry of each type. With this setting, it will allow several industries of the same type in the same townShow signals: î€®2Select on which side of the track to place signalsOn the leftOn the driving sideOn the right0Show finances window at the end of the year: î€®2Show finances window at the end of the period: î€®If enabled, the finances window pops up at the end of each year to allow easy inspection of the financial status of the companyIf enabled, the finances window pops up at the end of each period to allow easy inspection of the financial status of the company)New orders are 'non-stop' by default: î€®Á9Normally, a vehicle will stop at every station it passes. By enabling this setting, it will drive through all station on the way to its final destination without stopping. Note, that this setting only defines a default value for new orders. Individual orders can be set explicitly to either behaviour nevertheless;New train orders stop by default at the î€® of the platformÁ_Place where a train will stop at the platform by default. The 'near end' means close to the entry point, 'middle' means in the middle of the platform, and 'far end' means far away from the entry point. Note, that this setting only defines a default value for new orders. Individual orders can have their stop location set by clicking on the order textnear endmiddlefar end)Pan window when mouse is at the edge: î€®ZWhen enabled, viewports will start to scroll when the mouse is near the edge of the windowDisabledMain viewport, full-screen onlyMain viewportEvery viewport)Allow bribing of the local authority: î€® Allow companies to try bribing the local town authority. If the bribe is noticed by an inspector, the company will not be able to act in the town for six months¡Allow companies to try bribing the local town authority. If the bribe is noticed by an inspector, the company will not be able to act in the town for six minutes,Allow buying exclusive transport rights: î€®ŒIf a company buys exclusive transport rights for a town, opponents' stations (passenger and cargo) won't receive any cargo for twelve monthsIf a company buys exclusive transport rights for a town, opponents' stations (passenger and cargo) won't receive any cargo for twelve minutesAllow funding buildings: î€®=Allow companies to give money to towns for funding new houses,Allow funding local road reconstruction: î€®kAllow companies to give money to towns for road re-construction to sabotage road-based services in the town+Allow sending money to other companies: î€®=Allow transfer of money between companies in multiplayer mode;Weight multiplier for freight to simulate heavy trains: î€®‚Set the impact of carrying freight in trains. A higher value makes carrying freight more demanding 